---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/intermediate/class-and-stratification">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson takes the foundational lesson Society and Socialisation for granted:
    you already know what socialisation is and how institutions shape behaviour.
    Here the question sharpens into stratification — why the positions people are
    socialised into are unequal, how that inequality is measured, and how it
    reproduces itself across generations. Comfortable percentage-reading is the only
    technical requirement; every sociological term is built on the page.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define stratification and distinguish closed systems (caste, estate) from open ones;</li>
    <li>contrast Marx's relational and Weber's multidimensional definitions of class;</li>
    <li>read the standard instruments — the Gini coefficient, top shares, intergenerational earnings elasticity — and the Great Gatsby curve;</li>
    <li>separate absolute from relative mobility, and explain why education raises one but not the other;</li>
    <li>explain Bourdieu's three capitals and how education reproduces class while genuinely helping individuals.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Stratification is <strong>how societies sort people into unequal positions — and how the
    sorting persists</strong>. The persistence is the sociological puzzle: individuals clearly
    rise and fall, yet across generations the overall shape of the distribution barely
    budges, and your starting rung predicts your children's rung with uncomfortable accuracy.
    Sociology's contribution is seeing class as <em>structure</em>, not individual outcome:
    the rungs, ladders and escalators exist before anyone climbs, and each theoretical
    tradition — Marx's production, Weber's markets, Bourdieu's culture — is a different
    claim about what holds the structure in place.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the definitions, the mobility curve, cultural capital, reproduction" widget="equationStep" config={classCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four cards — they are this
    page in miniature. Card 1 holds the two founding definitions you are about to
    meet in full; card 2 introduces the curve that ties inequality to mobility.
    Then watch how cards 3 and 4 answer a question cards 1 and 2 leave open: not
    where class positions come from, but how they are handed on.
  </p>

  <h2>What is stratification? Systems and the two founding definitions</h2>
  <p>
    All societies distribute resources unequally; stratification is when that inequality is
    <strong>structured, durable and passed on</strong>. The classic typology runs from
    <strong>closed</strong> to <strong>open</strong> systems: caste (ascribed at birth,
    marrying within, ideology of purity — still detectable in Indian marriage and labour
    data despite legal abolition), estate (legal rank in feudal Europe: clergy, nobility,
    commoners), and class (nominally open, defined by economic position, entered by
    "achievement"). Modern sociology's working definitions descend from two rivals. For
    <strong>Marx</strong>, class is relational: defined by one's position in production —
    those who own the means of production (bourgeoisie) versus those who must sell their
    labour (proletariat). Class is therefore a structural antagonism, not a ladder. For
    <strong>Weber</strong>, that captures only part of it: <em>class</em> is market position —
    your skills and assets in the labour market, which set your <strong>life chances</strong>
    (the odds of good health, housing, education); <em>status</em> is honour and prestige
    (which can run against money — the professor and the plutocrat); <em>party</em> is
    organised power. Weber's multidimensional scheme survives in official class scales
    (occupation-based indices like the NS-SEC or Goldthorpe schema), and the two traditions
    still map the field: Marx explains where the antagonisms come from; Weber explains why
    the sorting looks like several overlapping ladders rather than one.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file the systems by openness" widget="sort-buckets" config={stratSystemsSort} />
  <p>
    The standard functionalist counter-argument (Davis and Moore, 1945) held that
    stratification exists because society must reward scarce, important roles — surgeons
    must be paid more than messengers or no one would train. The critique (Tumin, and
    later evidence) is withering: the reward system pays for access to training as much as
    for talent, reproduces the accessors' advantages, and misprices — care work and
    sanitation, functionally vital, sit at the bottom of the pay scale. Functioning
    societies clearly can retain incentive structures with far less spread; the level of
    inequality is a choice the Davis-Moore logic cannot itself explain.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="walk the defence and the rebuttal" widget="branch" config={stratDMBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> class is the same as income. Income is a flow; wealth
  (accumulated assets, housing, inheritance) is a stock — and wealth is far more
  concentrated than income everywhere (the top 10% hold roughly half to three-quarters of
  national wealth in most rich countries, versus a third to a half of income). Two
  families with identical salaries can occupy different classes if one owns capital and
  the other does not — which is precisely Marx's point.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="fill the flow-versus-stock distinction" widget="cloze" config={stratWealthCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> class is a structure that predates any individual —
    caste, estate and class systems sort people with increasing openness, and Marx
    and Weber still frame every modern argument about what the sorting is. Next:
    how sociologists actually measure the sorting and the movement through it.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring inequality and mobility</h2>
  <p>
    The instruments: the <strong>Gini coefficient</strong> (0 = perfect equality, 1 = one
    person owns everything; Nordic countries run around 0.27-0.30, the UK and US around
    0.37-0.41 for income, South Africa around 0.63), <strong>top-share statistics</strong>
    (the income share of the top 1%, the Piketty-Saez long-run series), and
    <strong>deprivation measures</strong> that track what households actually lack.
    Mobility takes two forms that must never be confused: <strong>absolute mobility</strong>
    (are you richer in money terms than your parents — usually yes in growing economies)
    versus <strong>relative mobility</strong> (does your <em>rank</em> in the distribution
    match your parents' rank — sticky everywhere). The headline statistic is the
    <strong>intergenerational earnings elasticity</strong> — how much of a 1% difference in
    parental earnings shows up in the child's: around 0.45-0.50 in the US and UK, around
    0.15 in Denmark and Norway. Corak's <strong>Great Gatsby curve</strong> plots exactly
    these against each other: more unequal countries are less mobile — wider gaps make
    each rung farther away, and the advantages money can buy (housing in good school
    catchments, tutoring, unpaid internships) scale with the gap. Chetty's big-data
    mobility studies (tax records for the entire US population) sharpened the point
    geographically: mobility varies enormously between US counties, and neighbourhood —
    even the same city block in childhood — moves adult outcomes, with the Opportunity
    Atlas letting researchers read the effect almost experimentally.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="place the steepest national figure on the scale" widget="place" config={stratGiniPlace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "mobility is rising because everyone is richer
  than their parents." That is absolute mobility, and growing economies deliver it
  almost automatically. The sociologically decisive question is relative mobility —
  whether your <em>rank</em> matches your parents' rank — and by that measure
  position-holding has barely moved in decades. Most heated mobility arguments are
  two people talking about different ladders.</p>

  <p>
    Education's own double ledger deserves the sharpest statement, because it carries
    most policy hope. <strong>Expansion raises absolute mobility</strong> — every cohort
    is more schooled than its parents, genuinely — yet <strong>relative</strong> class
    gaps in degree completion have been stubbornly flat in country after country
    (Breen and Goldthorpe's comparative analyses are the standard citation): as
    middle-class participation hits saturation, working-class gains close the
    <em>proportion</em> gap slowly or not at all, because the competition re-runs
    at the next level (postgraduate study, elite institutions, unpaid internships).
    The "maximally maintained inequality" hypothesis names the pattern: expansion
    helps first those the previous round left out, then stalls. It is the sociological
    reason "more university places" keeps under-delivering on the mobility promise
    while early-childhood and school-quality investments keep over-delivering.
  </p>

  <h2>Bourdieu: capital, habitus and the reproduction machine</h2>
  <p>
    Bourdieu's intervention explains <em>how</em> advantage crosses generations without
    anyone conspiring. He distinguishes three forms of <strong>capital</strong>: economic
    (money and assets), social (networks and who will vouch for you), and
    <strong>cultural capital</strong> — embodied familiarity with the dominant culture's
    codes: vocabulary, tastes, ease with institutions, what counts as "sounding smart".
    These are learned in the family as the <strong>habitus</strong> — durable dispositions
    that feel like personality but are class biography. The machine runs through
    education, which appears neutral ("merit") while evaluating middle-class fluency:
    Bourdieu's <em>Distinction</em> (1979) showed taste itself stratifying — preferences
    for "difficult" art, certain sports, restrained speech function as
    <strong>distinction</strong>, marking rank while insisting it is only quality. The
    empirical receipts: class gaps in school attainment open before schooling begins,
    widen over the summer holidays when school is absent, and survive generations of
    reform; Bernstein's sociolinguistics traced how "elaborated" school codes match
    middle-class home codes; credential inflation (Collins) then devalues each
    qualification wave, forcing ever-longer queues of schooling to reach the same jobs.
    Education genuinely equalises at the margin — every extra year of schooling helps
    individuals — while reproducing at the aggregate; both things are true, which is why
    "more education" is always both a real escalator and a disappointing fix.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each form with its definition" widget="match" config={stratCapitalsMatch} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> school is a pure meritocracy, so class gaps in
  attainment simply reflect talent and effort. Bourdieu's point is not that effort
  is fake — every extra year of schooling genuinely helps individuals — but that
  the "merit" being measured includes cultural capital learned at home, so the
  race does not start level. Education equalises at the margin and reproduces at
  the aggregate; both things are true at once.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the reproduction machine — capitals converted at home,
    certified as merit at school, and credential inflation resetting the queue. If
    that were the whole story, nothing would ever change; the next section shows
    inequality is also policy-responsive.
  </p>

  <h2>What has been tried: the compression experiments</h2>
  <p>
    Inequality is policy-responsive; history proves it. The <strong>Great Compression</strong>
    (US, 1940s-1970s): top income shares fell steeply under progressive wartime taxation,
    unionisation, and the GI Bill, and stayed low for decades — then re-concentrated from
    the 1980s as top tax rates fell and unions receded, on the trajectory Piketty and Saez
    documented country by country. The <strong>welfare state</strong> is the standing
    machinery: Esping-Andersen's <em>Three Worlds of Welfare Capitalism</em> (1990)
    distinguishes liberal regimes (means-tested, market-dominant — US), conservative
    regimes (insurance-based, family-centred — Germany) and social-democratic regimes
    (universal, <strong>decommodifying</strong> — making wellbeing less dependent on market
    position — Sweden). The Nordic evidence: universal services plus progressive taxation
    compress the distribution before and after earnings, and high equality coexists with
    high growth — refuting the assumption that compression costs dynamism. Specific
    levers with evidence behind them: progressive taxation and inheritance/wealth taxes
    (attacking the stock, not just the flow); minimum wages (modest compression with
    limited disemployment at moderate levels — Card and Krueger's New Jersey study and
    its successors); early-childhood provision (the highest return per dollar in
    education policy — Heckman's longitudinal analyses of preschool programmes); and
    universal basic services or income pilots (Finland's 2017-18 UBI trial improved
    wellbeing with small employment effects — a real result, modest in scope). Affirmative
    action and targeted place-based policies attack the compounding axes. The honest
    verdict from the comparative data: no single lever does it; the low-inequality
    countries stack many levers, and the mobility they buy comes mostly from removing
    price tags from childhood (healthcare, childcare, education) rather than from
    heroic ladders.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="find the faulty line — the compression record" widget="spot-error" config={stratCompressionSpot} />

  <h2>Globalisation: inequality between societies, the other direction</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's third question reverses the camera: what has globalisation done to
    inequality <em>between</em> societies? The surprise is that the trend turned. For two
    centuries the industrial revolution made nations diverge; since about 1990,
    <strong>between-country inequality has fallen for the first time in the modern
    era</strong>, driven by catch-up growth in China and India — China alone lifted about
    800 million people out of extreme poverty after 1978, and the global extreme-poverty
    rate fell from roughly 38% in 1990 to under 10% by the late 2010s. Milanovic's
    <strong>elephant curve</strong> (global income growth 1988-2008) shows the
    distribution reshaping: the global middle — mostly emerging-Asian households — gained
    50-80%, and the global top 1% gained nearly as much in absolute terms, while the
    lower-middle of the <em>rich-world</em> distribution stagnated. Decomposed:
    <strong>between-country inequality fell; within-country inequality rose</strong> in
    most OECD states. The mechanisms are the standard trade-offs of global integration:
    global value chains moved low-skill work to low-wage regions (converging wages
    between countries, pressuring them within), capital became hypermobile (tax
    competition, the "race to the bottom" that empirical tax studies show is real but
    partial), and winner-take-all markets went global — the same star CEO, singer or
    software platform now serves the whole world market. Standing's <strong>precariat</strong>
    names the labour-market residue: gig work, zero-hour contracts, unstable schedules —
    insecurity that once defined the bottom now creeps upward through the middle class.
    And stratification has gone partly transnational: a global elite of education,
    passports and tax residences can arbitrage jurisdictions, while the global poor face
    borders — the sharpest stratification line of all (Milanovic's "citizenship premium":
    where you are born predicts lifetime income more than anything you can do about it).
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="estimate the catch-up scale" widget="estimate" config={stratChinaEstimate} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A country's economy grows so much that nearly everyone is richer than their parents, yet nobody's position in the distribution has changed. What has risen, and what has stayed flat?</li>
    <li>A student arrives at university already fluent in its unspoken norms — how to speak in seminars, what to aspire to. In Bourdieu's terms, what are they deploying, and why does the institution mistake it for merit?</li>
    <li>State the Davis-Moore functionalist defence of stratification and the sharpest line of Tumin's critique.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Absolute mobility — money income — has risen;
    relative mobility, your rank against your parents' rank, is unchanged. Growing
    pies lift incomes without moving ladders. (2) Cultural capital: embodied
    familiarity with the dominant culture's codes, learned in the family and
    certified by the school as "ability" — Bourdieu's misrecognition. (3) Davis and
    Moore: inequality exists because society must reward scarce, important roles,
    or no one would train for them. Tumin: the reward system pays for access to
    training as much as for talent, reproduces the accessors' advantages, and
    misprices vital work — care and sanitation sit at the bottom of the pay scale.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Stratification is structured, durable, inherited inequality — not an individual outcome.</li>
    <li>Marx defines class by relation to production; Weber by market position, status and party.</li>
    <li>Wealth is a stock, income a flow — and wealth is far more concentrated everywhere.</li>
    <li>Absolute mobility rises with growth; relative mobility (rank) is sticky in every country measured.</li>
    <li>The Great Gatsby curve: more unequal countries are less mobile.</li>
    <li>Bourdieu: cultural capital, learned at home, is certified by schools as merit.</li>
    <li>Inequality is policy-responsive: the low-inequality countries stack many levers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Class intersects with every other sorting device a society runs — the sibling
    lessons Gender &amp; Society and Race, Ethnicity &amp; Identity treat those axes
    directly, and Deviance &amp; Crime asks who gets labelled when the structure
    cracks. After those, the advanced lessons take Piketty's r-versus-g argument
    apart in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>r versus g.</strong> Piketty's <em>Capital in the Twenty-First Century</em>
    (2013) rests on one inequality: when the return on capital (r, historically 4-5%)
    exceeds economic growth (g, recently 1-2%), accumulated wealth compounds faster than
    wages grow, and inherited wealth reconcentrates without anyone breaking a rule. The
    thesis is contested — capital returns can fall as wealth piles up, growth can
    surprise upward — but the data series behind it (top wealth shares since 1800) is
    now the field's longest telescope, and the inheritance data confirms the mechanism
    running again in France, Germany and the US.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="drag r above g and watch inheritance run away" widget="plotter" config={stratRgPlotter} />
  <p>
    <strong>Class identity lags class position.</strong> In surveys across rich countries,
    large majorities call themselves "middle class" at almost every income level — the
    subjective-objective gap. Identity is relative and local (compared to neighbours, not
    national deciles), which makes class politics unstable and populism legible: parties
    can win by re-labelling who "the elite" is, even as objective intergenerational
    persistence grinds on.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Will remote work and AI diffuse opportunity
    geographically, or recentralise it to superstars? Does the falling between-country
    curve survive the first truly global decade of climate adaptation costs? And can
    cultural capital be genuinely equalised — or does any institution that signals merit
    immediately acquire a market in the signals? The one settled lesson of the field:
    every society produces stratification, but the form, steepness and justifying
    ideology are made, not given — and therefore changeable.
  </p>
